          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The four winds primarily denote the four cardinal directions and therefore worldwide extent or universality, with contextual associations such as judgment, divine agency, or life-giving breath.\",\"book_summary\":\"The four winds are identified as four heavenly spirits—God’s executing agents—specifically correlated with the four horsemen, and their fourfold outgoing pattern is also associated with Eden’s divided river.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Four compass quarters signifying universal geographic scope.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Four personal or spiritual executing agents of God, identified with the four horsemen.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus applies the expression broadly wherever four winds indicate all directions, including gathering, scattering, judgment, and breath. The book instead reclassifies it as a particular set of four heavenly agents and extends their pattern to the Eden river, excluding mere directional or universal-scope uses from its core identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"For the term’s central referent, geographic directions or universal extent are not the same thing as four spiritual agents. Accepting the book’s identification as the term’s core replaces the consensus identification, although heavenly agents were recognized by the consensus as a minority contextual alternative.\",\"rationale\":\"This is not novel because the blind responses explicitly list heavenly spirits or angelic agencies, especially from Zechariah 6:5, as an alternative. Nevertheless, the book promotes that alternative into the defining referent, identifies the agents specifically with Revelation’s four horsemen, and links them to Eden’s fourfold river pattern. It therefore replaces rather than merely narrows the consensus core.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Zechariah 6:5 supports four heavenly spirits associated with four chariots, but the printed passages do not explicitly identify them with Revelation 6’s horsemen. Genesis 2:10 establishes a river divided into four heads, while Revelation 22:1 describes one river and does not explicitly connect either river with the four spirits or horsemen.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
